      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the area of the shaded region. The shaded region is formed by subtracting the area of the sectors from the areas of the semicircles.\n\nFirst, let's identify the areas we need to calculate:\n1. The area of the semicircle with radius 1.\n2. The area of the sector \\( \\angle ADB \\) that lies above the diameter \\( AE \\).\n\nThe area of a semicircle with radius 1 is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of semicircle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi r^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi (1)^2 = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the area of sector \\( \\angle ADB \\). Since \\( \\angle ADB = 90^\\circ \\) (because \\( D \\) and \\( E \\) are the midpoints of the arcs \\( ADB \\) and \\( BEC \\) respectively, and \\( AE \\) is the diameter of the semicircle with radius 1), the sector is \\( 90^\\circ \\) and it is a quarter circle.\n\\[\n\\text{Area of sector } \\angle ADB = \\frac{1}{4} \\pi r^2 = \\frac{1}{4} \\pi (1)^2 = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n\\]\n\nThe area of the shaded region is the area of the semicircle minus the area of the sector \\( \\angle ADB \\):\n\\[\n\\text{Area of shaded region} = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the shaded region is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\n\\]",
